    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 21st with a condensed slate, forcing us to lean heavily on our high-efficiency anchors. **Kazuma Okamoto** leads our charge today with a 64.92 score, buoyed by a favorable platoon advantage against Rod\u00f3n. The combination of **Kazuma Okamoto** hitting cleanup and the SP's 4.33 xERA creates an environment where we expect significant R and RBI production. Similarly, **Carson Benge** (60.83) serves as our primary table-setter out of the leadoff spot; his ability to exploit Cavalli's 3.99 xERA makes him an essential start, particularly as we look to manufacture baserunners in a high-variance matchup. While **Dillon Dingler** (60.45) is also a strong play, his value is predicated heavily on the dynamic platoon advantage against Cantillo; if the lineup shifts against a righty, we would see his efficiency rating crater instantly.\n\nWith **Yordan Alvarez**, **Junior Caminero**, **Trea Turner**, and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** all sidelined due to team off-days, our bench depth is effectively neutralized today. The most difficult \"miss\" in our projections remains the lack of high-end stolen base threats available for this specific rotation. We are closely monitoring the status of **Caleb Durbin**, **Hyeseong Kim**, and **Zac Veen**\u2014given our organization's tactical emphasis on SB scarcity, these players remain the primary depth options for rotating into the lineup if we need to sacrifice a marginal HR floor for speed. For today, we are effectively locked into our current starters, as no other bench pieces like **Kyle Schwarber**, **Christian Yelich**, **Colt Keith**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Luke Raley**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Josh Smith**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Luis Pena**, or **Pedro Ramirez** hold active games to challenge the starting trio. Risks remain localized to potential late-inning defensive substitutions for **Dillon Dingler**, but otherwise, we are positioned to maximize the available efficiency points in a thin daily market.",
